    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. i think it fits the home office deduction since i only use that room for design work. but i also had some smaller stuff like coffee for clients and some random software subscriptions. is there like a limit on how much i can claim for those small stuff items, or can i just dump them all into one category?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You can absolutely categorize those smaller items, but you shouldn't just \"dump\" them all into one bucket if you want to keep your books clean. Think of it like this: if you buy a pile of assorted snacks for a client meeting, that's a distinct expense from a recurring software subscription. It's best to keep the coffee for clients separate from the monthly Adobe subscription.\n\nFor the software, keep it under \"Software Subscriptions\" or \"Technology Expenses.\" For the client coffee, \"Meals & Entertainment\" (though be mindful of current IRS rules on that one) or \"Client Entertainment\" is appropriate.\n\nThe IRS doesn't impose a specific dollar limit for *categorizing* these small items, but maintaining clear records for each type of expense is crucial for an audit. A messy ledger is worse than a slightly complicated one!",
      "source": "target",
